WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:05.350
Kenya was known as the Silicon Savanna,

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:07.780
name coming from the Silicon Valley.

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:11.360
A lot of early adopters
in the tech space started

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.870
creating a community and building products

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:17.700
that has now led to the creation
of the African tech space.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.110
After over a decade of development,

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:22.690
Africa's startup scene is booming.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:26.510
Last year, the tech sector
attracted a record $5 billion,

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:28.200
more than the total amount raised

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.100
in the previous three years combined.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.370
It's actually kind of
mind blowing in many ways.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:36.510
The pace of growth in
the last couple years

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.380
is incredible in terms
of the amount of funding

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.477
coming into the continent.

00:00:40.477 --> 00:00:41.910
And that is particularly

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:45.770
because there was a lot
of interest and excitement

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:47.980
from U.S. investors.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:51.760
We saw some really scaling
up their investments.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.780
The continent also saw the creation

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.430
of a record number of five unicorns

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:59.213
private companies valued over $1 billion.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:03.143
And this year looks even more promising.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:07.440
The creation of unicorns
that has more or less

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.960
proved to investors that you
can make African tech companies

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.990
into multibillion-dollar companies

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:16.170
that are profitable on the continent.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:17.980
It's a vibrant place to be.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:19.770
We've got great innovators

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:23.180
and we are beginning to
see that evolve quickly.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:24.540
But Africa's startup scene

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:28.070
is still much smaller compared
to the rest of the world.

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:31.100
With regulation differences
and income disparities

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:35.273
across the continent, can
African startups keep growing?

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:49.630
When you talk of the
Genesis of tech in Africa,

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:52.640
you'd have to go back.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:55.597
When government started
promoting the internet

00:01:55.597 --> 00:01:57.850
and the use of the internet.

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:01.710
We started to see a pipeline
of software engineers

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:02.860
that came out.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:05.590
And some of the things
that they started doing

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:09.300
was really making
applications, for instance,

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:14.240
HR management applications for
universities, for hospitals,

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.890
for large stores, and factories.

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:20.150
Also in the 1990s, big tech companies

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:22.300
brought cellphones to the continent,

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:26.180
laying foundations for
Africa's mobile internet age.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:27.680
Kenya has been one of the hubs

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.610
leading Africa's tech
innovations since the 2000s.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:35.720
You can argue that Nairobi is
where Africa's tech started.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:37.680
So in the late 2000s

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.260
with the advent of the sea cables coming

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.790
onto the coast of Kenya
and with the availability

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:45.920
of well-educated, young people,

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.120
a community was created in Nairobi.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.520
And iHub was one of the
first community spaces

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.543
on the continent that brought
innovation groups together.

00:03:01.227 --> 00:03:02.710
Founded back in 2010

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:05.140
by a group of like-minded techies,

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:08.110
iHub provided a much-needed
space for entrepreneurs

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:12.520
in Nairobi to meet, share
ideas and develop products.

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:15.290
The first three years of iHub was amazing.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:20.250
There was this energy that
was on another level of people

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:22.670
to actually chat with each other,

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:24.920
people to build prototypes.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:29.300
People having hackathons,
hackathons week after week,

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:33.000
just to see who could
develop the best app.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:34.180
Training is happening.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:37.700
People giving their time to
train the young developers.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:41.960
So it was really intense of
how amazing the community was

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:46.870
from all levels, academia from
corporate, from government,

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:49.293
to tech community itself.

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:53.950
We've had hundreds of
startups that came out

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:55.770
of the iHub ecosystem.

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:58.890
Some have actually been very successful.

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.610
Since its inception iHub has helped create

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:06.610
150 startups and created
more than 2500 jobs.

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:09.210
Achieving what the Kenyan
government tried to

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:10.193
but couldn't do.

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:14.320
You see world-class office space.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:18.280
We see launchpads to
extraordinary startups

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:20.830
and profitable organizations.

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:24.190
In 2008, two years before iHub was born,

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:27.150
the state proposed a
plan to build a tech hub

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:29.330
called Konza Technopolis,

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:32.810
about 60 kilometers southeast of Nairobi.

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:34.220
But over a decade later,

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:36.930
the project is still a work in progress.

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:39.580
You cannot force innovation to happen.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:41.350
It's not just the space that you need.

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:42.930
It's the people that come with it.

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:45.993
It's the talent you need
to build this tech space.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:51.880
Another milestone in Kenya's tech history

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:54.100
was the birth of M-Pesa,

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:56.763
a mobile phone-based
money transfer service.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:00.320
It was launched in 2007 by Safaricom,

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.430
Kenya's biggest telecom company

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:05.363
partly owned by the UK firm Vodafone.

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:09.540
Like many African countries,
Kenya's banking system

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:13.220
was underdeveloped and a huge
majority of the population

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:14.890
didn't have a bank account.

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:17.800
That meant transferring
money was difficult.

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:20.170
We started with the send
money home proposition,

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:23.500
because Kenya has a
rural to urban migration

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:25.634
and everyone in the urban
areas are sending money

00:05:25.634 --> 00:05:26.920
to rural areas.

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:30.040
And that meant taking time
off work or over the weekend

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:32.040
to go see your parents
and give them money.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:36.230
I remember in 2007, I used to
travel almost 500 kilometers,

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:38.880
from Nairobi to go give my mom money.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:42.060
Just with the advent of the
send money home proposition,

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:45.700
that changed how we send
money in Kenya dramatically.

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:48.480
But smartphones hadn't
arrived yet in Kenya

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:53.150
and so the M-Pesa team relied
on USSD and SIM toolkit,

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:56.783
the same technology
used for sending an SMS.

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:02.430
Users registered for an M-Pesa
account with their SIM cards,

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:05.840
put cash into the account
through an M-Pesa agent

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:08.970
and then sent the money by phone.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:12.250
Recipients could also
cash out through an agent.

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:14.100
The transaction fee for sending money

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:17.663
was 30 Kenyan Shillings, about 26 cents.

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:23.910
It went on and was developed
into a complex payment system

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:27.750
where people can not
only just transfer money,

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:32.210
but can also pay bills, borrow.

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:35.650
And they're now even investment
products that are on M-Pesa,

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:39.654
In fact, even the Kenyan
government used the system

00:06:39.654 --> 00:06:43.110
to sell bonds at one time.

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:46.050
We have 30 million active customers

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:48.740
on the M-Pesa Kenya ecosystem.

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:52.050
Today, well over 50% of
GDP of Kenya is actually

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:53.000
flowing through it.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:59.170
The success of M-Pesa
ushered in mobile money

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:02.860
across the continent, spawning
the birth of other startups

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:06.800
trying to solve everyday
problems in Africa.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:11.120
Among them Sendy, a Nairobi-based
digital logistics company

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:13.150
facilitating door-to-door deliveries

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:15.463
between individuals and businesses.

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:20.090
Logistics in Africa is quite
fragmented and quite costly.

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:22.960
If you have an item on
the shelf about $10,

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.850
about four to six dollars
of that is we can attribute

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:28.763
that to logistics, over
40% to 60% of that.

00:07:28.763 --> 00:07:31.270
And that's the problem
we sought out to solve,

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:33.270
reduce that to single digit form.

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:35.800
And technology has allowed
companies like Sendy

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:39.120
to aggregate the informal logistics sector

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:42.153
into a more homogenous kind of play

00:07:42.153 --> 00:07:44.780
that all these businesses
can actually trade.

00:07:44.780 --> 00:07:46.670
When we talk about shipping,

00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:48.710
you have to also talk about payments.

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:51.800
We started in Kenya because we kind of saw

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:53.850
how the digital economy
was growing in Kenya.

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:55.980
And the penetration we
had seen in mobile money

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:59.340
in this market enabled us
to move quite fast on that.

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:06.450
2021 was a breakout year

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:08.500
for startups like Sendy.

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:10.470
With the Covid-19 pandemic,

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:12.730
many consumers turned to online services

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:15.593
for grocery deliveries,
tutoring and healthcare.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:20.670
African startups raised a
record $5 billion last year.

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:23.320
And nearly two-thirds
of the top 20 largest

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:26.643
disclosed deals involved
investors from the US.

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:30.110
In 2016, Mark Zuckerberg visited Kenya

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:32.950
and he interacted with the tech space.

00:08:32.950 --> 00:08:37.180
In 2019, Jack Dorsey also
toured three, four countries

00:08:37.180 --> 00:08:39.950
on the continent, was also
planning on actually living

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:42.070
in Africa for six months.

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:43.840
The interest has been there.

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:47.210
What changed was that companies proved

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:51.210
one, that investors
can exit and make money

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:52.330
for their investment.

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:55.793
But secondly, that you're able
to scale on the continent.

00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:58.900
The money pouring into
Africa's tech sector

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:01.500
minted five unicorns last year,

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:03.750
a huge number for the ecosystem

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:06.890
given the continent only
has six unicorns so far

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:10.090
and four of those are Nigeria-based.

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:15.060
As far as monetization of tech
and startups is concerned,

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:16.830
Nigeria came in a little bit later,

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:21.400
but has had the hugest success
in terms of monetization.

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:23.450
The African unicorns that we have now

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:27.270
are mainly in Nigeria
because there's money

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:30.210
and an economy to be mobilized there.

00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:32.554
One of those unicorns is Flutterwave,

00:09:32.554 --> 00:09:36.130
a fintech startup facilitating
cross-border payments.

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:40.120
Africa specifically was
a very complicated market

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:42.360
from a digital payment perspective.

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:45.580
It was a very fragmented
market in the sense

00:09:45.580 --> 00:09:48.660
that we had over 50 countries.

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:53.660
We have to date, about 40
currencies in circulation

00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:56.260
and over 300 types of digital payments.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:10:00.100
And so what Flutterwave
does is pull all of those

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:02.660
different payment types
across the countries,

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:05.810
across currencies onto one platform.

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:08.490
So that as an individual or as a business,

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:13.090
it becomes much easier for
you to reach your customers

00:10:13.090 --> 00:10:17.530
or for you to send peer-to-peer
transfers to family members,

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:19.884
friends in other geographies.

00:10:19.884 --> 00:10:24.884
Flutterwave caused a lot
of excitement in 2021.

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:27.270
It had not just become a unicorn,

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:30.000
but it became a unicorn in five years,

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.710
which was a quite short period of time.

00:10:32.710 --> 00:10:35.159
And its founder, Agboola,

00:10:35.159 --> 00:10:38.775
really became a go-to person
on how to do it in Africa,

00:10:38.775 --> 00:10:41.942
really became a go-to person
on how to do it in Africa,

00:10:41.942 --> 00:10:44.560
We are focusing on building new products

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:47.250
that will make a very positive impact

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:50.450
on the life of everyone that
comes across those products.

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:53.273
In fact, that's what fintech means to us.

00:10:54.460 --> 00:10:58.240
Today over 900,000
businesses use Flutterwave

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:01.050
and over 200 million
individual transactions

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:03.550
have been processed on the platform.

00:11:03.550 --> 00:11:06.270
Nigeria is the company's biggest market

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:10.263
but it has ambitious to expand
in Africa and even go global.

00:11:11.170 --> 00:11:13.910
We're starting with Egypt and Morocco.

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:17.080
And beyond that, we're also
looking to build a base

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:18.490
outside of Africa.

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:21.220
We're looking at Europe,
we're looking at the U.S.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:24.380
Eventually, we'll have our
eyes on Canada as well.

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:27.357
So it's really exciting times for us.

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:33.850
But compared with the rest of the world,

00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:37.250
African startups are still
much smaller in size.

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:39.360
And while money piles into the continent's

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:42.520
four big tech hubs,
startups in smaller markets,

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:44.590
including those run by women,

00:11:44.590 --> 00:11:46.793
are still struggling to secure funding.

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:50.700
Even with a lot of capital coming in,

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:53.640
again, it's chasing very
specific industries,

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:54.473
certain markets.

00:11:54.473 --> 00:11:56.900
So if you're kind of not in
those industries, markets

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:58.610
or necessarily have the networks,

00:11:58.610 --> 00:12:01.430
which is one of the challenges
a lot of women have.

00:12:01.430 --> 00:12:04.310
There's a significant difference
between how much money

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:08.310
is going to all-male founders versus teams

00:12:08.310 --> 00:12:10.900
that have women founders or all women.

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:13.030
But we're really talking
about single digits.

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:14.700
We're talking about single digits

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:18.220
of all that investment
actually going to women-led

00:12:18.220 --> 00:12:19.713
and women-owned businesses.

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:23.530
We're in a continent
where our digital destiny

00:12:23.530 --> 00:12:25.210
is what we're focused on.

00:12:25.210 --> 00:12:28.350
And so how do you really think
about adjusting and ensuring

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:30.220
this more equitable distribution

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:33.360
of the economic gains
that we made from this.

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:35.940
Lack of basic infrastructure
and regulations

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:38.700
also create problems for African startups,

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:40.730
those that are looking
to develop their business

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:43.330
and scale up across the continent.

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:45.650
The basics still need work.

00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:49.770
For example, in Nigeria, the
need to have access to energy

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:54.030
is something that the government
needs to help facilitate.

00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:57.360
Similarly in terms of road
and railway, in majority

00:12:57.360 --> 00:12:59.410
of the continent,

00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:01.730
are huge investments that
still need to be done.

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:04.540
So that startups that are
working say in logistics

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:08.050
are able to kind of plug into that system.

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:10.588
Regulation is different
across these markets.

00:13:10.588 --> 00:13:13.890
Regulation in Kenya is quite
different from regulation

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:15.237
in other countries that we dealing.

00:13:15.237 --> 00:13:19.020
And having to deal with
that is quite painful

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:21.240
for a business like us.

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:23.690
Some of the fintechs innovate and then go

00:13:23.690 --> 00:13:26.940
to the regulator or innovate
and not aware of the laws.

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:29.490
You're seeing some of the
countries rolling up tax laws

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:32.150
that are targeting such
an emerging industry

00:13:32.150 --> 00:13:34.490
where it's e-commerce and so on.

00:13:34.490 --> 00:13:37.395
In the nascent stages
of growth, when you tax,

00:13:37.395 --> 00:13:40.223
that could limit the potential of it.

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:44.710
While the continent is large
we have a lot of people

00:13:44.710 --> 00:13:47.800
on mobile, you have more
people using the internet.

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:51.890
The reality is that it can
be hard to get a good grasp

00:13:51.890 --> 00:13:54.410
about the size of the consumer base

00:13:54.410 --> 00:13:56.780
that you can really monetize.

00:13:56.780 --> 00:13:59.120
We certainly are in a market
with a lot of inequity

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:02.100
and don't have necessarily
the purchasing power

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:05.033
at an average consumer base
that some other markets have.

00:14:08.660 --> 00:14:10.260
But starting up in Africa

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:12.350
still has its advantages.

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:15.360
The continent has the youngest
population in the world,

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:20.150
with 70% of sub-Saharan
Africa under the age of 30.

00:14:20.150 --> 00:14:21.810
Young Africans provide the continent

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:23.770
with a rich talent pool.

00:14:23.770 --> 00:14:26.650
Africa also benefits from a vast diaspora,

00:14:26.650 --> 00:14:30.710
an estimated 40 million
people in North America alone.

00:14:30.710 --> 00:14:34.440
As someone who was educated
in the US, and the first wave,

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:38.100
I would say when I came back
and many diaspora before me

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:41.970
was coming back to the continent
and physically being here,

00:14:41.970 --> 00:14:44.650
starting companies or
working at places like Google

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:46.470
where I worked, the tech companies

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:48.470
that were setting up on the continent.

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:50.810
I think over some time,
what I was starting to see

00:14:50.810 --> 00:14:53.530
as a shift is that there's
a lot of opportunities

00:14:53.530 --> 00:14:56.170
for the diaspora to not move back,

00:14:56.170 --> 00:14:59.660
but to play a role in different ways.

00:14:59.660 --> 00:15:02.520
So diaspora who are
investing in tech companies,

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:04.250
some who are advising tech companies,

00:15:04.250 --> 00:15:06.270
and I think opens up more opportunities

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:08.860
for this collaboration of diaspora

00:15:08.860 --> 00:15:10.680
with those on the continent.

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:12.080
The potential in Africa,

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:14.040
we are just still scratching the surface

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:15.920
and we'll be seeing a lot more growth

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:17.370
in the next five to 10 years.

00:15:18.421 --> 00:15:20.600
We are going to have more companies

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:24.880
that are valued at two billion,
four billion, five billion,

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:26.960
10 billion, and even more.

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:30.930
We are going to see some
initial public offerings

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:32.793
from some of these companies.

00:15:33.830 --> 00:15:36.040
Tech startups are already
playing a big role

00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:37.720
on the continent.

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:39.000
They're creating employment.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:41.070
They're creating solutions to problems

00:15:41.070 --> 00:15:43.770
that are faced by the regular citizenry.

00:15:43.770 --> 00:15:46.330
They are providing innovation
that's making ability

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:49.680
to do business easier,
cheaper and more efficient.

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:51.600
In the next few years,
just gonna get better

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:53.750
and easier to do business
on the continent.

